        <type>std::string_view</type>
        <definition>static std::string_view iceberg::LocationUtil::ParseScheme</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::string_view location)</argsstring>
        <name>ParseScheme</name>
        <qualifiedname>iceberg::LocationUtil::ParseScheme</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::string_view</type>
          <declname>location</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Extract the URI scheme from a location. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>This follows Java&apos;s <ref refid="classiceberg_1_1ResolvingFileIO" kindref="compound">ResolvingFileIO</ref>: the text before the first colon is the scheme; an empty result means that no scheme was found. Text that is not syntactically a scheme (RFC 3986) yields no scheme, and on Windows a single letter is a drive rather than a scheme. The rest of the location is not validated. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="iceberg/util/location_util.h" line="41" column="27"/>
      </memberdef>
      <memberdef kind="function" id="classiceberg_1_1LocationUtil_1a54da92b737859f8eb2450d87ea69f94f" prot="public" static="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>bool</type>
        <definition>static bool iceberg::LocationUtil::HasScheme</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::string_view location)</argsstring>
        <name>HasScheme</name>
        <qualifiedname>iceberg::LocationUtil::HasScheme</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::string_view</type>
          <declname>location</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Whether a location starts with a URI scheme. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>This is the spec&apos;s test for an absolute path (see &quot;Paths in Metadata&quot;): the text before the first colon must be a non-empty RFC 3986 scheme. It is strict on every platform: unlike ParseScheme, a single letter such as <computeroutput>C:</computeroutput> counts as a scheme. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
